Drive the Marvell network coprocessor on Sophos XGS appliances.

On these boards every front port belongs to a Marvell coprocessor behind a PCIe endpoint, and
the host cannot reach a single one of them until that coprocessor has been brought up and
talked to. Two families are covered and they are not the same silicon.

On ARMADA boards - the XGS 136 and its siblings, a CN9131 - the coprocessor comes out of
power-on held in reset by an MCP2210 USB-to-SPI bridge whose factory pin state is "held". The
vendor firmware releases it during its own startup. Without that step the coprocessor never
boots, its serial console never says a word, and the appliance has no front ports at all. What
this package installs is that release, as an early boot hook.

On OCTEON TX boards - the XGS 3300, a Cavium CN83XX - the coprocessor boots on its own, and the
work is the protocol: an endpoint handshake, a facility mailbox, and the control plane that
raises a front port and reads its link back.

The release pin values are per board model, and they come from the vendor tool's own table
rather than from the bridge. Only AMDA0201 - the XGS 126 and 136 - has been tested on hardware,
and the plugin assumes that board rather than reading the assembly number back out of the
bridge's EEPROM. It does nothing at all on a machine that has no such bridge, but it should not
be installed on another XGS model until that lookup exists.

It also ships two endpoint drivers as source. npuep brings up the XGS 136's fourteen front
ports, and is installed and loaded by a boot hook. octep completes the OCTEON TX handshake and
gives the XGS 3300 its twelve front ports; it is built on the appliance by the installer, rebuilt
by a kernel follower after every kernel update, and loaded at boot by a hook that brings the ports
up before OPNsense configures them. It is never unloaded: its handshake runs once per coprocessor
boot and must never be re-armed.

